{
  "track": "mission",
  "allocation_reasoning": "M1's epistemic infrastructure is now complete: confidence scoring rubrics for all three claim types (F0009, F0023-F0027), cross-lab review protocols (F0013-F0015), and mathematical limits of knowability (F0016-F0022) are established and uncontested. F0010 has been resolved. The foundation is solid for advancing to M2 (Deductive and Mathematical Core) with a concrete, bounded task that yields machine-checkable anchor points. Resource work is not needed this cycle (0% of cycles so far, well under the 20% ceiling).",
  "roadmap_ops": [
    {"op": "set_status", "id": "M2", "status": "active", "reason": "Epistemic infrastructure (M1) is complete. Starting M2 to establish machine-checkable mathematical foundations that will serve as immutable dependencies for all subsequent work."},
    {"op": "set_status", "id": "T4", "status": "active", "reason": "T4 is a concrete, achievable task: formalize core theorems of Peano and Presburger Arithmetic with proof-assistant references. This directly builds on the mathematical limits catalogued in F0016-F0022 and provides verified deductive anchors."}
  ],
  "task_id": "T4",
  "task_brief": "Document essential arithmetic invariants (commutativity, associativity, distributivity, induction schema for PA; decidability of Presburger arithmetic) with formal proof-assistant references (Lean 4, Coq, Isabelle/HOL). For each theorem: state the theorem precisely, specify the formal system, cite the proof-assistant library/file where it is mechanically verified, assign confidence per F0009, provide refutation condition, and list builds_on dependencies. Target: 5-8 core theorems covering the bedrock of arithmetic. Deliver as proposed findings ready for cross-lab review.",
  "why_this_task": "T4 is the first task of M2 and directly serves its goal: 'Anchors the knowledge base in proof-checked deductive certainty that holds across all possible universes.' The mathematical limits work (F0016-F0022) established what cannot be decided; T4 establishes what can be mechanically verified as true. These findings will become immutable dependencies for all future formal work in M2 and beyond. The task is bounded, achievable in one session with arxiv_search and web_search for proof-assistant library references, and produces verifiable findings per Constitution Rule 4."
}

{
  "claim_reviews": [
    {
      "claim": 1,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Correct statement of addition commutativity and associativity in PA/mechanical natural numbers. Type 'verifiable' is correct (mechanically verified per F0009). Confidence 1.0 is correct per F0009 (mechanically verified in Lean 4, Coq, Isabelle/HOL). Refutation condition is operationally specific (kernel error or derivation of counterexample). Library citations are accurate (Lean: Init.Data.Nat.Basic, Coq: Coq.Arith.PeanoNat, Isabelle: HOL.Nat). Builds_on F0003 (supported, 1.0) and F0009 (supported, 0.95 ≥ 0.95, no C1 downgrade). No scope overreach or other downgrades apply."
    },
    {
      "claim": 2,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Correct statement of multiplication commutativity and associativity. Type 'verifiable' correct. Confidence 1.0 correct (mechanically verified). Refutation condition operationally specific. Library citations accurate. Builds_on F0009 and claim 1 (addition properties used in multiplication proofs). Dependency chain is sound. No downgrades apply."
    },
    {
      "claim": 3,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Correct statement of left and right distributivity. Type 'verifiable' correct. Confidence 1.0 correct. Refutation condition operationally specific. Library citations accurate (Lean: Nat.left_distrib/Nat.right_distrib, Coq: Nat.mul_add_distr_l/Nat.mul_add_distr_r, Isabelle: add_mult_distribute/add_mult_distribute2). Builds_on claim 1 and claim 2 correctly (distributivity proofs use addition/multiplication properties). No downgrades."
    },
    {
      "claim": 4,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Accurately describes the first-order induction schema and its higher-order formalization as structural induction/eliminators in proof assistants. Type 'verifiable' correct (the eliminators are mechanically defined and their correctness is kernel-trusted). Confidence 1.0 correct. Refutation condition operationally specific (counterexample to induction or kernel unsoundness). Sources correct (Lean: Nat.recOn/Nat.rec, Coq: nat_ind, Isabelle: nat_induct). Builds_on F0009 only. The distinction between first-order schema and higher-order primitive is properly acknowledged."
    },
    {
      "claim": 5,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Correct statement of Presburger arithmetic decidability with verified decision procedures. Type 'verifiable' correct. Confidence 1.0 correct (Isabelle/HOL has fully verified decision procedure in Presburger.thy; Coq's Lia and Lean's Omega are verified tactics for the decidable fragment). Refutation condition operationally specific (proof of undecidability or halting problem reduction). Sources accurate (Presburger 1929, Chaieb & Nipkow 2005 for Isabelle, Coq.micromega.Lia, Lean Mathlib.Tactic.Omega). Builds_on F0001, F0009, F0017 (all supported). No downgrades."
    },
    {
      "claim": 6,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Correct statement of Euclidean division existence and uniqueness. Type 'verifiable' correct. Confidence 1.0 correct. Refutation condition operationally specific (counterexample pair). Library citations accurate (Lean: Nat.div_add_mod/Nat.mod_lt, Coq: Nat.div_mod/Nat.mod_upper_bound, Isabelle: div_mult_mod_eq/mod_less_divisor). Builds_on chain (F0009, claims 1-4) is sound - division algorithm proofs use induction and arithmetic properties. No downgrades."
    },
    {
      "claim": 7,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Correct statement of Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic (unique prime factorization). Type 'verifiable' correct. Confidence 1.0 correct (mechanically verified in all three systems). Refutation condition operationally specific. Lean and Isabelle citations accurate. Coq citation 'Coq.ZArith.Zprime' refers to integer prime factorization; the natural number version is in Coq.Arith.Prime or Coq.Numbers.NatInt.NZFactorization - this is a minor citation inaccuracy but does not affect the truth of the claim (the theorem IS mechanically verified in Coq for naturals). Builds_on chain (claims 1-4, 6) is correct - unique factorization depends on division algorithm (claim 6) and induction. No downgrades warranted for a citation module name error when the verification exists."
    }
  ],
  "update_reviews": [],
  "task_complete": true,
  "appeal_check": "No claim appears shaped to impress rather than to be true. All statements are precise, mathematically standard, with accurate mechanical verification references. Confidence 1.0 is justified by F0009 for mechanically verified results. No overstatement of scope or significance beyond what the formal verifications establish.",
  "disagreements": [
    "Minor: Claim 7's Coq source citation 'Coq.ZArith.Zprime' refers to integer (Z) prime factorization; the natural number version is in Coq.Arith.Prime or Coq.Numbers.NatInt.NZFactorization. The verification exists, but the module path is slightly off."
  ],
  "next_step": "All 7 claims accepted as proposed findings. They establish core arithmetic invariants (addition/multiplication algebra, induction, Presburger decidability, Euclidean division, unique factorization) with mechanical verification references across Lean 4, Coq, and Isabelle/HOL. Ready for recording as supported findings."
}
